<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arechis II of Benevento</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/787">787</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Arechis II was duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Benevento</a> from 758. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> kingdom in 774, he took the title of prince, claiming to carry on the Lombard tradition in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. He fortified Salerno and built the church of Santa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofia">Sofia</a> in Benevento.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Arechis was born around 734, a descendant of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> who had invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in the late 6th century. Their kingdom had its capital at Pavia and included two southern duchies, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spoleto">Spoleto</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Benevento</a>. He was the son of Duke Liutprand, whom he succeeded in 758. Around 757 he married Adelperga, a daughter of the Lombard king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/desiderius">Desiderius</a>, and kept friendly but largely independent relations with his father-in-law until Desiderius was deposed in 774. The couple had five children, three boys and two girls.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Arechis refused to submit when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> made himself king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> in 774, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Benevento</a> was left largely alone. Taking the title of prince and issuing laws, which only Lombard kings had done before, were probably gestures of defiance. In 776 he was probably involved in a Lombard conspiracy against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friuli">Friuli</a>, but gave it little practical support. He fought the Byzantine duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> for territory, then, perhaps in the mid-780s, made a pactum with its duke on landholding and the settlement of disputes.</p><p>In 787 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> marched south and besieged Capua. Arechis withdrew to Salerno and, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/einhard">Einhard</a>, sent his sons Rumold and Grimold with a great sum of money as hostages, promising obedience on condition that he need not appear in person. Charlemagne kept the younger son. The Beneventan mint began striking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins like the Frankish ones, and coins and charters added Charlemagne's name and title to those of Arechis. Frankish influence was short-lived: after the deaths of Arechis and his elder son, Grimoald III was released and soon resisted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Arechis and Adelperga were notable patrons of culture. Adelperga commissioned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-the-deacon">Paul the Deacon</a>'s Historia Romana, a textbook of Roman history widely used through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, and some historians argue that the couple also commissioned his Historia gentis Langobardorum. Arechis moved saints' relics to the new church of Santa Sofia, which survives, heavily restored, with rare 8th-century frescoes. He built a palace and castle at Salerno, which grew into an important port, and endowed Montecassino and San Vincenzo al <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volturno">Volturno</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Arechis II of Benevento” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When Charlemagne took the north in 774, <b>Arechis II</b> took the title of prince and claimed the Lombard heritage, building the church of Santa Sofia.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-benevento">Duchy of Benevento</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Salerno, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.68,14.76&amp;year=777" aria-label="Salerno on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Arechis II of Benevento"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5947 4031h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5947 4031h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=40.68,14.76&amp;year=777">What was here in 777 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
